      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what? i just wanted to understand the math. why are you talking about goblins? can you just explain the actual frequency decomposition part without the weird analogies?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the excessive goblin metaphors earlier; I clearly got carried away with the \"goblin protocol\" and forgot that you just wanted the math intuition without the weird analogies.\n\nLet's strip it back to the core concept: The Fourier Transform works by assuming that *any* complex signal can be built up from a sum of simple sine and cosine waves.\n\nThink of it like this: A complex signal is a superposition of many different frequencies. The Fourier Transform acts as a filter that isolates each of these individual frequency components. It calculates the **amplitude** (how strong that frequency is) and the **phase** (where that wave starts in its cycle) for every single frequency present in your signal.\n\nMathematically, it projects your time-domain signal onto an infinite set of orthogonal basis functions (sines and cosines). By doing this, it tells you exactly which \"ingredients\" (frequencies) are needed to reconstruct the original signal perfectly. If you know the amplitude and phase of every frequency component, you can mathematically synthesize the original signal back in the time domain.\n\nSo, instead of seeing a messy time plot, you are looking at a spectrum that shows you the specific frequencies",
      "source": "target",
